from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "7. First Corinthians 14:1–19: Tongues and Prophecy Compared"),
("p", "Claim: Paul compares tongues and prophecy in the first half of the fourteenth chapter and prefers prophecy because the church is edified by what it understands. He describes tongues as speech that no man understands, spoken to God, and he asks for an interpreter. The volume reads the passage in order, reports the KJV’s italics and lowercase letters, and draws the sentences on private prayer into the same account of tongues."),
("sub", "7.1 Verses 1 to 5: speaking to God and speaking to men"),
("p", "Paul begins, “Follow after charity, and desire spiritual gifts, but rather that ye may prophesy” (1 Corinthians 14:1). He gives the reason in contrasting sentences. “For he that speaketh in an unknown tongue speaketh not unto men, but unto God: for no man understandeth him; howbeit in the spirit he speaketh mysteries” (1 Corinthians 14:2). “But he that prophesieth speaketh unto men to edification, and exhortation, and comfort” (1 Corinthians 14:3). The two gifts are set by their hearers. The tongue speaks to God, and no man understands it. The prophet speaks to men, and the end is edification, exhortation and comfort."),
("p", "Then: “He that speaketh in an unknown tongue edifieth himself; but he that prophesieth edifieth the church” (1 Corinthians 14:4). And: “I would that ye all spake with tongues, but rather that ye prophesied: for greater is he that prophesieth than he that speaketh with tongues, except he interpret, that the church may receive edifying” (1 Corinthians 14:5). The exception, “except he interpret,” makes the tongue equal to prophecy when it is interpreted. The measure throughout is the edifying of the church."),
("sub", "7.2 Verses 6 to 12: the comparison to sounds"),
("p", "Paul puts the case of his own coming: “Now, brethren, if I come unto you speaking with tongues, what shall I profit you, except I shall speak to you either by revelation, or by knowledge, or by prophesying, or by doctrine?” (1 Corinthians 14:6). The four ways of speaking that profit are revelation, knowledge, prophesying and doctrine. He illustrates with the pipe, the harp and the trumpet: “how shall it be known what is piped or harped?” (1 Corinthians 14:7); “For if the trumpet give an uncertain sound, who shall prepare himself to the battle?” (1 Corinthians 14:8)."),
("p", "Then the application: “So likewise ye, except ye utter by the tongue words easy to be understood, how shall it be known what is spoken? for ye shall speak into the air” (1 Corinthians 14:9). And the comparison to languages: “There are, it may be, so many kinds of voices in the world, and none of them is without signification. Therefore if I know not the meaning of the voice, I shall be unto him that speaketh a barbarian, and he that speaketh shall be a barbarian unto me” (1 Corinthians 14:10, 11). The illustration moves from instruments to the speech of nations. The tongue is compared to a voice in the world with signification, and the hearer who does not know it is a barbarian to the speaker. The volume reads the comparison as Paul’s own account of what tongues are: speech that has meaning and that cannot be understood without the language or the interpreter."),
("sub", "7.3 Verses 13 to 17: praying and singing"),
("p", "Paul applies the point to prayer. “Wherefore let him that speaketh in an unknown tongue pray that he may interpret” (1 Corinthians 14:13). “For if I pray in an unknown tongue, my spirit prayeth, but my understanding is unfruitful” (1 Corinthians 14:14). “What is it then? I will pray with the spirit, and I will pray with the understanding also: I will sing with the spirit, and I will sing with the understanding also” (1 Corinthians 14:15). And he sets the case of the assembly: “Else when thou shalt bless with the spirit, how shall he that occupieth the room of the unlearned say Amen at thy giving of thanks, seeing he understandeth not what thou sayest? For thou verily givest thanks well, but the other is not edified” (1 Corinthians 14:16, 17)."),
("p", "These sentences are the ones on which an argument for a private prayer language has been built. The volume reads them in their place. They stand in a passage whose subject is what is said in the assembly and whether it is understood, and they are joined by “wherefore” to the call for an interpreter. Paul speaks of praying in a tongue and then of praying with the understanding also, and he gives the reason in verses 16 and 17: the unlearned cannot say Amen to what he does not understand. The gift described is the same tongue of verses 2 and 4, and the sentences are about the same tongue prayed in the assembly. The volume does not find in the passage a separate gift of private prayer, and it reports the lowercase “spirit” of verses 14 and 15 without deciding whether the word names the Holy Spirit or the speaker’s own spirit. Study 81 reports the same verses and takes no position, and the volume cites it."),
("sub", "7.4 Verses 18 and 19: Paul’s own practice"),
("p", "Paul adds, “I thank my God, I speak with tongues more than ye all” (1 Corinthians 14:18). He gives the other side at once: “Yet in the church I had rather speak five words with my understanding, that by my voice I might teach others also, than ten thousand words in an unknown tongue” (1 Corinthians 14:19). Paul says he speaks with tongues, and he says more than the Corinthians. He does not say where, and he sets against it the rule of the church. The verse is evidence that the gift was in his own hands, and it falls under the volume’s rule on events of Acts 9 to 28: the gift is reported, not assigned to a single program."),
("tbl", "First Corinthians 14:1–19 in order.", [
    ("1 Corinthians 14:2–4", T("1 Cor 14:2–4"), "The tongue speaks to God and no man understands; the prophet speaks to men to edify the church."),
    ("1 Corinthians 14:5", T("1 Cor 14:5"), "Prophecy is greater, except that an interpreted tongue edifies."),
    ("1 Corinthians 14:10, 11", T("1 Cor 14:10, 11"), "Tongues are compared to kinds of voices in the world."),
    ("1 Corinthians 14:14, 15", T("1 Cor 14:14, 15"), "Praying and singing in a tongue, with the understanding also."),
    ("1 Corinthians 14:18, 19", T("1 Cor 14:18, 19"), "Paul’s own practice, and his rule for the church."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Tongues are speech to God that no man understands, compared by Paul to the kinds of voices in the world; prophecy is speech to men that edifies.",
    "An interpreted tongue edifies the church, and the point of the whole passage is understanding.",
    "The sentences on praying and singing in a tongue speak of the same tongue and are joined to the call for an interpreter.",
    "The KJV’s italic unknown and lowercase spirit are reported and nothing is built on them.",
    "Paul speaks with tongues more than the Corinthians, and he prefers five words with understanding in the church.",
  ]),
]
